### 318 E-Commerce - End-to-End API walkthrough (IntelliJ HTTP Client)
#
# How to use this file:
#   - Each block starting with "###" is one request. Click the green run
#     triangle in the gutter next to a request's method line (POST/GET/...)
#     to send just that one request - or use the "Run All Requests in File"
#     button at the top of the editor to run them in order automatically.
#   - Requests are numbered and MUST be run top-to-bottom the first time:
#     each one captures a value (token, id) into a variable with the
#     "> {% client.global.set(...) %}" script block below it, and later
#     requests reference that variable as {{variableName}}.
#   - Re-running from the top is safe except: user registration (#5) will
#     fail with 409 on a second run (email already exists) - either change
#     the email in #5, or just skip #5/#6 and reuse a token from an earlier
#     run (client.global variables persist across runs in the same IDE
#     session, viewable via the "environment" icon in the HTTP Client tool
#     window).
#   - Needs all five services running (ports 8081-8085) AND Kafka up via
#     `docker compose up -d` - #9/#12 specifically only pass once Kafka is
#     actually delivering events between order-service/inventory-service/
#     payment-service. Watch http://localhost:8090 (kafka-ui) while running
#     this to see "order-created", "stock-updated" etc. messages arrive live.

### 1. Login as the seeded admin (see AdminAccountInitializer)
POST http://localhost:8081/api/v1/users/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "admin@318ecommerce.local",
  "password": "Admin@12345"
}

> {%
    client.test("login succeeded", () => response.status === 200);
    client.global.set("adminToken", response.body.token);
%}

### 2. Create a product as admin (P4) - needed before we can place an order for it
POST http://localhost:8082/api/v1/products
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "name": "Mechanical Keyboard",
  "description": "End-to-end test product",
  "price": 79.99,
  "categoryId": "CAT-ELECTRONICS"
}

> {%
    client.test("product created", () => response.status === 201);
    client.global.set("productId", response.body.id);
%}

### 3. Give it stock as admin (I3) - auto-creates the StockItem since none exists yet
POST http://localhost:8084/api/v1/inventory/{{productId}}/adjust
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

{
  "delta": 50,
  "reason": "Initial stock for e2e test"
}

> {%
    client.test("stock adjusted", () => response.status === 200);
    client.test("quantity is 50", () => response.body.quantityAvailable === 50);
%}

### 4. Sanity check: stock is visible via I1 (no auth needed)
GET http://localhost:8084/api/v1/inventory/{{productId}}

### 5. Register a customer account (U1) - change the email if re-running this file
POST http://localhost:8081/api/v1/users/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "e2e-customer@test.local",
  "password": "Customer@123"
}

### 6. Login as that customer (U2)
POST http://localhost:8081/api/v1/users/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "e2e-customer@test.local",
  "password": "Customer@123"
}

> {%
    client.test("login succeeded", () => response.status === 200);
    client.global.set("customerToken", response.body.token);
%}

### 7. Browse products as a sanity check (P1, no auth needed)
GET http://localhost:8082/api/v1/products?page=0&size=20

### 8. Place an order for 2 units (O1) - this publishes OrderCreatedEvent to Kafka
POST http://localhost:8083/api/v1/orders
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

{
  "items": [
    { "productId": "{{productId}}", "quantity": 2 }
  ]
}

> {%
    client.test("order created", () => response.status === 201);
    client.test("status is PENDING", () => response.body.status === "PENDING");
    client.global.set("orderId", response.body.id);
%}

### 9. Check stock again (I1) - should now read 48 once inventory-service has
### consumed OrderCreatedEvent from Kafka. If this still shows 50, wait a
### second and re-run just this request - Kafka delivery isn't instant.
GET http://localhost:8084/api/v1/inventory/{{productId}}

### 10. Pay for the order (PAY1) - any method value except MOCK_CARD_DECLINE succeeds
POST http://localhost:8085/api/v1/payments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

{
  "orderId": "{{orderId}}",
  "amount": 159.98,
  "method": "VISA_TEST"
}

> {%
    client.test("payment created", () => response.status === 201);
    client.test("status is SUCCESS", () => response.body.status === "SUCCESS");
    client.global.set("paymentId", response.body.id);
%}

### 11. Check the order again (O2) - should now read PAID once order-service
### has consumed PaymentConfirmedEvent from Kafka. If it still shows PENDING,
### wait a second and re-run - same Kafka-delivery caveat as #9.
GET http://localhost:8083/api/v1/orders/{{orderId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

### 12. Check payment status by orderId (PAY2)
GET http://localhost:8085/api/v1/payments/order/{{orderId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

### 13. Admin view of all transactions (PAY4)
GET http://localhost:8085/api/v1/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 14. Cancel a *different* order to test refund eligibility - place one more order first
POST http://localhost:8083/api/v1/orders
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

{
  "items": [
    { "productId": "{{productId}}", "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}

> {%
    client.global.set("orderId2", response.body.id);
%}

### 15. Pay for order #2 so it becomes eligible for a refund after cancelling
POST http://localhost:8085/api/v1/payments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

{
  "orderId": "{{orderId2}}",
  "amount": 79.99,
  "method": "VISA_TEST"
}

> {%
    client.global.set("paymentId2", response.body.id);
%}

### 16. Cancel order #2 (O4) - needs order-service to have already processed
### #15's PaymentConfirmedEvent (order must be PAID, not PENDING) - if this
### 409s with INVALID_ORDER_STATE, wait a moment for Kafka and retry.
POST http://localhost:8083/api/v1/orders/{{orderId2}}/cancel
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

> {%
    client.test("cancelled", () => response.status === 200);
    client.test("status is CANCELLED", () => response.body.status === "CANCELLED");
%}

### 17. Request a refund for order #2's payment (PAY3) - should now be eligible
POST http://localhost:8085/api/v1/payments/{{paymentId2}}/refund
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{customerToken}}

{
  "amount": 79.99
}

> {%
    client.test("refund processed", () => response.status === 201);
    client.test("status is PROCESSED", () => response.body.status === "PROCESSED");
%}

### 18. Low-stock alerts as admin (I4) - probably empty unless you've pushed
### quantityAvailable below the default threshold (10)
GET http://localhost:8084/api/v1/inventory/alerts
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
